think HUNK4-6 built the index, the seen page, and the content-addressed sheet. The last named rung closes the sprite quest: let the index itself travel as text — the McMaster-Carr catalog crossing to a portable record and home again losing nothing. Pure Bron render/parse over sprite.SpriteIndex, mirroring tablecloth's format tablecloth-v1 idiom.
think A reader needs the entries (name + window) to render them. sprite.SpriteIndex's cells are private-by-convention, so I added two small additive pub accessors — len and entry(i) returning a pub Entry (name slice + Window) — rather than reaching into cell storage from another file. HUNK4 must re-run GREEN (additive), and it did.
think The crux worth proving: parse rebuilds over a GIVEN sheet through SpriteIndex.add, so every window is validated against the real sheet — a catalog can never name bytes the sheet lacks. Proven by parsing the same text over a smaller 4x4 sheet and refusing WindowOutOfBounds. Plus the fixed point render(parse(render(x))) byte-for-byte and the rebuilt index rendering the same windows.
obs GREEN on the first build and run. Studied tablecloth's render_manifest/parse_manifest for the flat-Bron idiom (header line, tokenizeScalar, no-slack extra-field refusal, parse_int for numbers) and matched it. Symlinked tally/parse_int.rye into image/ (tally_copy already there). tame_style_check exit 0, width exit 0; HUNK4 sprite re-run GREEN beneath.
obs The sprite-marketplace quest now stands whole end to end — HUNK4 index, HUNK5 seen page, HUNK6 content-addressed sheet, HUNK7 travels as text — four signed rounds, each its own green witness on both remotes, mirroring the HUNK0-3 Open Image quest close. One decode serves a whole catalog; the catalog itself is portable and self-validating against its sheet.
obs Additive throughout — sprite.rye grew two pub accessors, nothing else changed. No breach, so no cairn. No network, key, or funds — hosting a real sheet and sourcing parts stay custody gates #2/#3.
